===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|xno|abatre}}, probably an alteration of {{der|en|xno|-}} and {{der|en|frm|embatre}}, {{m|frm|enbatre|t=to drive or rush into; to enter into a tenement without permission}} (compare {{cog|la-lat|abatare}}), from {{der|en|frm|-}}, {{der|en|fro|em-}}, {{m|fro|en-|pos={{glossary|prefix}} meaning ‘in, into’}} + {{der|en|frm|-}}, {{der|en|fro|batre|t=to beat, hit, strike}} (from {{der|en|la|battere}}, {{m|la|battuere}}, the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|la|battuō|t=to beat, hit; to beat up; to fight}}; see further at [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]]). The English word was probably also influenced by the {{glossary|verb}} ''abate''.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''v.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=123|date=December 2020}}</ref>

