===Preposition===
{{en-head|prep}}

# [[physically|Physically]] over; on top of; worn on top of, said of clothing. {{defdate|first attested before 1150.}}
#: {{ux|en|He always put his coat on '''above''' his sweater.}}
# In or to a higher place; higher than; on or over the upper surface. {{defdate|first attested  before 1150}}
#: {{antonyms|en|below|beneath}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Genesis|1|20|text=And God said, Let the waters bring foorth aboundantly the mouing creature that hath life, and foule that may flie '''aboue''' the earth in the open firmament of heauen.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=Then came a maid with hand-bag and shawls, and after her a tall young lady. She stood for a moment holding her skirt '''above''' the grimy steps, {{...}} and the light of the reflector fell full upon her.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=May-June|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/william-e-conner-1 William E. Conner]
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/an-acoustic-arms-race An Acoustic Arms Race]
|volume=101|issue=3|page=206–7|magazine=w:American Scientist
|passage=Earless ghost swift moths become “invisible” to echolocating bats by forming mating clusters close (less than half a meter) '''above''' vegetation and effectively blending into the clutter of echoes that the bat receives from the leaves and stems around them.}}
# Farther north than. {{defdate|first attested before 1150}}
#: {{ux|en|Idaho is '''above''' Utah.}}
# Rising; appearing out of reach height-wise. {{defdate|first attested around 1150–1350}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Higher than; superior to in any respect; [[surpassing]]; higher in measure, degree, volume, or pitch, etc. than; out of reach; not exposed to; not likely to be affected by; incapable of negative actions or thoughts. {{defdate|first attested around 1150–1350}}
#: {{co|en|to cut '''above''' average}}
#: {{ux|en|Even the chief of police is not '''above''' suspicion.}}
#: {{ux|en|He was always '''above''' reproach.}}
#: {{ux|en|I thought you said you were '''above''' these kinds of antics.}}
#: {{ux|en|That's '''above''' my comprehension.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1594|author=w:Christopher Marlowe|title=Edward II|section=act I, scene i|passage=Thy worth, sweet friend, is far '''above''' my gifts,}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Acts|26|13|text=At midday, O king, I saw in the way a light from heaven, '''above''' the brightnes of the Sunne, shining round about mee, and them which iourneyed with me.}}
# Higher in rank, status, or position. {{defdate|first attested around 1150–1350}}
#: {{co|en|to stand head and shoulders '''above''' the rest}}
#* {{RQ:Critical Pronouncing Dictionary|page=578|ppage=1|passage=☞ This word [{{m|en|wrap}}] is often pronounced ''wrop'', rhyming with ''top'', even by ſpeakers much '''above''' the vulgar.}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} In addition to; [[besides]]. {{defdate|first attested around 1150–1350}}
#: {{co|en|'''above''' and beyond the call of duty}}
#: {{co|en|over and '''above'''}}
# [[surpassing|Surpassing]] in number or quantity; more than. {{defdate|first attested around 1350–1470}}
#: {{ux|en|That amount is way '''above''' our asking price.}}
# In preference to.
# Too [[proud]] to stoop to; [[averse]] to; [[disinclined]] towards.
#: {{ux|en|The owner was '''above''' taking more than a token salary.}}
# Beyond; on the other side.
# {{lb|en|theater}} [[upstage|Upstage]] of.

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|surpassing in number or quantity}} passing into the [[adverbial]] sense.

