#: {{syn|en|abstruse}}
# [[separate|Separately]] expressing a [[property]] or [[attribute]] of an object that is considered to be [[inherent]] to that object: [[attributive]], [[ascriptive]]. {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|attributive|ascriptive}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1843|author=w:John Stuart Mill|title={{w|A System of Logic|A System of Logic, Ratiocinative and Inductive}}|volume=1|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=y4MEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA34&dq=%22A+concrete+name+is+a+name+which+stands%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjE7KGZ6fraAhUBvbwKHV4pBYgQ6AEIJzAA#v=onepage&q=%22A%20concrete%20name%20is%20a%20name%20which%20stands%22&f=false|page=34
|passage=A concrete name is a name which stands for a thing; ''an '''abstract''' name which stands for an attribute of a thing''...}}
# [[pertain|Pertaining]] [[comprehensively]] to, or [[represent|representing]], a class or group of [[object]]s, as opposed to any [[specific]] object; considered apart from any application to a [[particular]] object: [[general]], [[generic]], [[nonspecific]]; [[representational]]. {{defdate|First attested by Locke in 1689.}}
#: {{syn|en|general|generalized|generic|nonspecific|representational}}
#: {{ant|en|discrete|specific|particular|precise}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1843|author=w:John Stuart Mill|title={{w|A System of Logic|A System of Logic, Ratiocinative and Inductive}}|volume=1|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=y4MEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA34&dq=%22A+concrete+name+is+a+name+which+stands%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjE7KGZ6fraAhUBvbwKHV4pBYgQ6AEIJzAA#v=onepage&q=%22A%20concrete%20name%20is%20a%20name%20which%20stands%22&f=false|page=34
|passage=A concrete name is a name which stands for a thing; an abstract name which stands for an attribute of a thing.{{...}}A practice, however, has grown up in more modern times, which, if not introduced by Locke, has gained currency from his example, of applying the expression ''"'''abstract''' name" to all names which are the result of abstraction and generalization, and consequently to all general names'', instead of confining it to the names of attributes.}}
#* {{Q|en|Laurence, Stephen and Margolis, Eric|Abstraction and the Origin of General Ideas,| Philosophers' Imprint volume 12, no. 19, December 2012|year=2012|quote=Given their opposition to innate ideas, philosophers in the empiricist tradition have sought to explain how the rich and multifarious ''representational'' capacities that human beings possess derive from experience. A key explanatory strategy in this tradition, tracing back at least as far as John Locke’s An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, is to maintain that the acquisition of many of these capacities can be accounted for by a process of '''''abstraction'''''. In fact, Locke himself claims in the Essay that '''''abstraction''' is the source of all general ideas'' (1690/1975, II, xii, §1). Although Berkeley and Hume were highly critical of Locke, '''''abstraction''' as a source of generality has been a lasting theme in empiricist thought''.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[absent-minded|Absent-minded]]. {{defdate|First attested in the early 16<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=VII|line=1099|passage='''Abſtract''' as in a tranſe methought I ſaw,<br>'''abstract''', as in a trance|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author=w:D. H. Lawrence| title={{w|Aaron's Rod (novel)|Aaron's Rod}}| passage=White and '''abstract'''-looking, he sat and ate his dinner.}}
# {{lb|en|arts}} Pertaining to the formal aspect of art, such as the lines, colors, shapes, and the relationships among them. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
## {{lb|en|arts|often|capitalized}} Free from representational qualities, in particular the non-representational styles of the 20<sup>th</sup> century. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
##* {{RQ:Huxley Crome Yellow|chapter=12|passage=But his design is wonderful. He’s getting more and more '''abstract''' every day. He’d given up the third dimension when I was there and was just thinking of giving up the second. Soon, he says, there’ll be just the blank canvas. That’s the logical conclusion. Complete abstraction.}}
## {{lb|en|music}} [[absolute|Absolute]].
## {{lb|en|dance}} Lacking a story.
