# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[separate]]; to disengage. {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:Scott Peveril of the Peak|passage=He was incapable of forming any opinion or resolution '''abstracted''' from his own prejudices.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[remove]]; to [[take away]]; [[withdraw]]. {{defdate|First attested in the late 15<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1834|author=w:Harriet Martineau| title=Illustration of Political Economy| volume=IX| passage=The lightning of the public burdens, which at present '''abstract''' a large proportion of profits and wages.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|euphemistic}} To [[steal]]; to take away; to [[remove]] without permission. {{defdate|First attested in the late 15<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1872|author={{w|William Black (novelist)|William Black}}|title=The Strange Adventures of a Phaeton
|passage=Von Rosen had quietly '''abstracted''' the bearing-reins from the harness.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=The Travels of a Hindoo to Various Parts of Bengal and Upper India| author=Bholanauth Chunder| year=1869| passage=The inlaid characters in diamond, and other precious stones, have been all '''abstracted''' away by the pelf-loving Jaut and Mahratta—leaving the walls defaced with the hollow marks of the chisel.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=A P Simester; J R Spencer; G R Sullivan|title=Simester and Sullivan's Criminal Law: Theory and Doctrine
|passage=Section 13 of the 1968 Act enacts a separate offence of dishonestly '''abstracting''' electricity. The separate offence is needed because electricity, like other forms of energy such as heat, is not property.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[extract]] by means of [[distillation]]. {{defdate|Attested from the early 17<sup>th</sup> century until the early 18<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1601|author=w:John Marston| title=Antonio's Revenge| passage=Poison from roses who could e'er '''abstract'''?| section=act II, scene I}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To draw off (interest or attention).
##* '''June 1869''', {{w|William Blackwood}}, ''Late for the Train'' (published in ''Blackwood's Magazine'')
##*: The young stranger had been '''abstracted''' and silent.
##: ''He was wholly '''abstracted''' by other objects.''
## {{lb|en|intransitive|reflexive|literally|figuratively}} To [[withdraw]] oneself; to [[retire]]. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[consider]] abstractly; to [[contemplate]] separately or by itself; to consider theoretically; to look at as a general quality. {{defdate|First attested in the early 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1781|title=w:The History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire| volume=II| author=w:Edward Gibbon| passage=To '''abstract''' the notions of time, of space, and of matter.}}
### To [[conceptualize]] an [[ideal]] [[subgroup]] by means of the [[generalize|generalization]] of an [[attribute]], as follows: by [[apprehend |apprehending]] an attribute [[inherent]] to one individual, then [[separate|separating]] that attribute and contemplating it by itself, then [[conceive|conceiving]] of that attribute as a general quality, then [[despecification|despecifying]] that conceived quality with respect to several or many individuals, and by then [[ideate|ideating]] a group composed of those individuals [[perceive|perceived]] to possess said quality.
### {{lb|en|intransitive|rare}} To perform the process of [[abstraction]].
###* {{quote-text|en|year=1710|author=w:George Berkeley|title=s:A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge
|passage=I own myself able to '''abstract''' in one sense.}}
### {{lb|en|intransitive|fine arts}} To create abstractions.
### {{lb|en|intransitive|computing}} To produce an [[abstraction]], usually by [[refactoring]] existing code. Generally used with "out".
###: ''He '''abstracted''' out the square root function.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[summarize]]; to [[abridge]]; to [[epitomize]]. {{defdate|First attested in the late 16<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>

