#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William C. Larrabee| chapter=Lecture X. Evidences of Design from the Structure and Adaptations of the External Senses.| editor=[[w:Benjamin Franklin Tefft|B[enjamin] F[ranklin] Tefft]]| title=Lectures on the Scientific Evidences of Natural and Revealed Religion| location=Cincinnati, Oh.| publisher=Published by L. Swormstedt & J. H. Power, for the {{w|Methodist Episcopal Church}},{{nb...|at the Western Book Concern, corner of Main and Eighth Streets.}}; R. P. Thompson, printer| year=1851| section=paragraph 233| page=177| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=R5o5AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA177| oclc=4596096| passage=The acuteness of sound in stringed instruments depends on three circumstances—length, thickness, and tension. The shorter, smaller, and tighter a string, the more '''acute''' the sound. {{...}} In the violin, when you desire an '''acute''' sound, you tighten the string. When you wish a loud sound, you draw the bow over the strings heavily.}}
