#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Fyodor Dostoevsky| tlr=w:Constance Garnett| chapter=Elders| title=w:The Brothers Karamazov| series=Novels of Fyodor Dostoevsky| seriesvolume=1| location=London| publisher=[[w:Heinemann (publisher)|W[illiam] Heinemann]]| year=1912| oclc=5234211| title2=The Brothers Karamazov| location2=New York, N.Y.| publisher2=w:Modern Library| year2=[1943]| page2=32| pageurl2=https://archive.org/details/brotherskaramaz00dost/page/32| oclc2=3216382| passage=It was at this time that the discord between Dmitri and his father seemed at its '''acutest''' stage and their relations had become insufferably strained.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Thomas Keneally| title=Shame and the Captives|location=North Sydney, N.S.W.| publisher=[[w:Random House|Random House Australia]]| year=2013| isbn=978-0-85798-099-1| edition2=1st Atria Books hardcover| location2=New York, N.Y.| publisher2=[[w:Atria Publishing Group|Atria]]| year2=2015| page2=87| pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=CWZOBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA87| isbn2=978-1-4767-3464-4| passage=Then, at three, for Neville's sake and for the sake of her marriage as undernourished and spectral as it had been rendered by absence, its substance being all in the future, and an honest hope of hearing some news or of extending solace to other women, not least those with children, who seemed each to have an '''acuter''' sense of the man she was missing than Alice had of Neville, she attended the Friday meeting for wives and mothers of prisoners of war at the School of the Arts.}}
