#* {{quote-book|en|author=G. J. Kaloyanides|chapter=Drug-induced Acute Renal Failure| editors=Rinaldo Bellomo; Claudio Ronco| title=Acute Renal Failure in the Critically Ill| series=Update in Intensitve Care and Emergency Medicine| seriesvolume=20| location=Berlin; Heidelberg| publisher=[[w:Springer Science+Business Media|Springer-Verlag]]| year=1995| page=204| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=kGHyCAAAQBAJ&pg=PA204|doi=10.1007/978-3-642-79244-1| isbn=978-3-642-79246-5| passage=Of particular relevance to the ICU [intensive care unit] setting is ketorolac, a NSAID [non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug] that is being increasingly used for pain control in order to avoid problems of respiratory depression, sedation, and addiction associated with narcotics. {{...}} ICU patients, who typically are under great stress from an '''acute''' illness that is often accompanied by multiorgan dysfunction including renal insufficiency, are especially prone to develop renal complications from ketorolac{{nb...}}.}}
