#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:William Dwight Whitney|William D[wight] Whitney]]|title=II.—On the Nature and Designation of the Accent in Sanskrit.|journal=w:Transactions of the American Philological Association|location=Hartford, Conn.|publisher=Published by the [[w:American Philological Association|[American Philological] Association]]; printed by Case, Lockwood & Brainard|year=1869–1870|year_published=1871|pages=40–41|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=c-w5AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA40|oclc=643390955|passage=There would be no sense in our assuming that even an independent circumflex after an '''acute''' might be raised in pitch for the sake of clearer distinction from that '''acute'''; for it is sufficiently distinguished by its sliding tone; and, if it had any right to be further distinguished, an '''acute''' following an '''acute''' would have much more right; while, nevertheless, any number of '''acutes''' are allowed to succeed one another, without modification of their natural character.}}
