===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|adamantine}}, {{m|enm|adamantyne}}, {{m|enm|adamauntyn|t=(''adjective'') of adamant; (''noun'') adamant}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=adama(u)ntīn|pos=adj|id=MED446}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|adamantin}} and {{der|en|frm|adamantin|t=of or resembling adamant or diamond}} (modern {{cog|fr|adamantin}}), and from its {{lg|etymon}} {{der|en|la|adamantinus|t=adamantine}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀδᾰμάντῐνος|t=hard as adamant; made of steel}}, from {{m|grc||ᾰ̓δᾰμᾰντ-}} (a {{lg|stem}} of {{m|grc|ἀδάμᾱς|t=the hardest metal (probably steel); diamond}}, possibly originally {{der|en|sem}}) + {{m|grc|-ῐνος|pos={{lg|suffix}} meaning ‘made of’ forming {{lg|adjective}}s}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n.'' and ''adj|id=6276778179|date=December 2025|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}</ref> {{surf|en|adamant|-ine|pos2=suffix meaning ‘of or pertaining to’ forming adjectives}}.

{{senseno|en|magnetic|uc=1}} (“having the quality of attracting or drawing”) and {{senseno|en|bright}} (“like diamond in lustre; etc.”) refer to {{m|en|adamant|t=(''archaic'') lodestone; (''historical, poetic'') diamond}}.

